  description="CAV research pillars — Ethno-Ethics, Sacred Axiology, Semantic Ethics, Chronological Ethics — and intended methods: Algorithmic Hermeneutics, Evolutionary Game Theory, Normative Synthesis."
>
  <PageHeader
    kicker="Research program"
    title="Map variation. Test the baseline."
    lede="CAV studies whether humanity shares a unified moral grammar or whether values are fundamentally relative to context. This page states pillars, intended methods, and provisional hypotheses — not completed findings."
  />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-12 sm:px-8">
    <article class="measure prose-cav text-[1.05rem]">
      <h2>What counts as an anomalous virtue</h2>
      <p>
        An anomalous virtue is a moral quality, strength, or excellent trait that is
        unusual, unexpected, or seemingly contradictory in its context. Candidates
        include untranslatable culture-words (central under Semantic Ethics);
        philosophical and ethical cases such as honor among thieves, flawed heroes, and
        contextual virtues; and literary or character-design patterns such as the
        sympathetic villain or the reluctant savior. The phrase is not a single classical
        ethics term. It is a research designation at the intersection of philosophy,
        literature, and related discourse.
      </p>
      <p>
        A concept is anomalous not because it is exotic, but because it does not sit
        inside the portable English of values training — respect, kindness, dignity,
        tolerance — without remainder.
      </p>

      <h2>Five research pillars</h2>
      <p>
        Four analytic pillars structure the program. Practice is the applied strand —
        detailed on its own page — and must not overclaim what research has established.
      </p>

      <h3>1. Cultural &amp; Geographical Variations (Ethno-Ethics)</h3>
      <p>
        How moral expectations shift with landscape, resource scarcity, honor versus
        dignity cultures, and collectivist versus individualist social forms. Geography
        and demography are treated as conditions of ethics, not decorations on it.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Religious &amp; Theological Foundations (Sacred Axiology)</h3>
      <p>
        Comparative study of divine-command ethics, karmic and immanent moral cosmologies,
        and secular humanist frameworks — as distinct architectures for grounding
        obligation, not as interchangeable “faith perspectives.”
      </p>

      <h3>3. Linguistic Relativity in Morals (Semantic Ethics)</h3>
      <p>
        Untranslatable virtues — Ubuntu, Filotimo/Philotimo, Chesed, and others — and the
        grammatical framing of blame, praise, and duty. Where a word will not sit still in
        English, we treat the remainder as evidence. This is where we keep
        <em>precise bridges where generic language fails</em>.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Temporal &amp; Historical Evolution (Chronological Ethics)</h3>
      <p>
        Long-run change in virtue vocabularies: Axial Age convergences; the shift from
        warrior ideals through Enlightenment civic virtues to pressures characteristic of
        digital public life. History is a variable, not a museum.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Practice (applied engagement)</h3>
      <p>
        Training, diplomacy, and institutional work that apply comparative insight without
        certifying competence or selling national character. See
        <a href="/practice">Practice</a>. Practice follows research standing.
      </p>

      <h2>Intended methods</h2>
      <p>
        The following are methods we intend to develop and apply. They are not claims that
        the work is finished or that results already exist.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Algorithmic Hermeneutics.</strong> Computational reading of corpora —
          philosophical, religious, literary, and ethnographic — to surface patterns in
          moral vocabulary and argument, always returned to human interpretation and
          community correction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Evolutionary Game Theory.</strong> Formal models of cooperation,
          punishment, and reputation as tools for probing whether recurring “moral
          grammar” elements (fairness, harm reduction, in-group loyalty) can be explained
          as stable strategies under selective pressure — without reducing ethics to
          fitness alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Normative Synthesis.</strong> Careful comparison across pillars aimed at
          provisional synthesis: what, if anything, survives as shared baseline once
          contextual overlays are accounted for — and what must remain irreducibly local.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Expertise informing, not ruling</h2>
      <p>
        A hybrid AI–human research center inherits a standing risk: that specialized
        expertise, once amplified by computational scale, becomes a quiet substitute for
        public judgment. We treat that risk as methodological, not merely political.
        Expertise remains essential for complex comparative work; anti-intellectual
        rejection of specialists is not a research program. The target of critique is
        <em>technocracy</em> — expertise deployed as a warrant to rule — rather than
        expertise offered to inform.
      </p>
      <p>
        Accordingly, methods that surface patterns must return interpretation to citizens,
        speakers, and practitioners whose lived experience and common sense are moral
        ground, not residual noise. Credentialist echo chambers and accountability
        deficits are failure modes we intend to name and resist. Artificial intelligence
        here facilitates, deepens, and scales open inquiry among people; it does not
        replace human judgment or install a machine technocracy.
      </p>

      <h2>Working hypotheses <span class="font-serif text-base font-normal text-muted">(provisional)</span></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Moral Baseline.</strong> An evolutionary “moral grammar” — recurring
          concerns such as fairness, harm reduction, and in-group loyalty — may be shared
          across human populations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contextual Overlayer.</strong> Expression of that baseline is shaped by
          language, geography, historical trauma, theology, and institutional form —
          producing the variation that invites relativist conclusions.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These hypotheses orient the program. They will be revised or abandoned as evidence
        requires. We will not invent citations to make them look settled.
      </p>

      <h2>Working method (field discipline)</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Attest, don’t harvest.</strong> Record how a term or practice is used by
          speakers and practitioners before interpreting it. Unpublished or restricted
          knowledge stays unpublished.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep competing accounts.</strong> Disagreement inside a language or
          tradition is data. A single “authentic” gloss is often a political act
          pretending to be a dictionary.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Mark the seam.</strong> State what a portable English nearly says, then
          what it cannot. That seam is an object of study.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate illustration from claim.</strong> Catalog entries are
          sourced and under ongoing review. Formal papers will carry sources.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Return draft to source.</strong> Communities named in an entry review
          language, withhold what they choose, and may withdraw cooperation.
        </li>
      </ol>


      <h2>Studies</h2>
      <p>
        Working papers and longer comparative essays appear here as they are ready for
        public release after director sign-off.
      </p>
